=========================================================
RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) – Interview Notes
=========================================================

1. What is RAII?
----------------
RAII is a C++ programming idiom where a resource is acquired in a constructor
and automatically released in the destructor.

Resource lifetime is tied to object lifetime.

Introduced:
- Core C++ idiom (not a language feature).
- Used extensively in modern C++.

---------------------------------------------------------

2. Why RAII?
------------
Without RAII:
• Manual resource management.
• Memory leaks.
• Resource leaks (files, sockets, mutexes).
• Exception unsafe.

With RAII:
• Automatic cleanup.
• Exception safe.
• No resource leaks.
• Cleaner code.

---------------------------------------------------------

3. Resources Managed by RAII
----------------------------
• Heap memory (new/delete)
• Files (fopen/fclose)
• Mutexes (lock/unlock)
• Database connections
• Network sockets
• Threads (join via wrapper)
• Any OS resource

---------------------------------------------------------

4. RAII Example (Memory)
------------------------

class Resource
{
    int* ptr;

public:
    Resource()
    {
        ptr = new int(100);
        cout << "Memory Allocated\n";
    }

    ~Resource()
    {
        delete ptr;
        cout << "Memory Released\n";
    }
};

int main()
{
    Resource obj;
}

Output:
Memory Allocated
Memory Released

Destructor automatically frees memory.

---------------------------------------------------------

5. RAII During Exceptions
-------------------------

void func()
{
    Resource obj;

    throw runtime_error("Error");
}

Even if an exception is thrown,
the destructor is called automatically,
so resources are released.

This is why RAII provides exception safety.

---------------------------------------------------------

6. RAII with Smart Pointers
---------------------------

unique_ptr<int> p = make_unique<int>(10);

No delete required.

When p goes out of scope,
memory is automatically released.

Smart pointers are the best example of RAII.

---------------------------------------------------------

7. RAII with Mutex
------------------

mutex m;

void func()
{
    lock_guard<mutex> lock(m);

    // Critical Section

} // lock_guard destructor unlocks mutex automatically.

Without RAII:
m.lock();
// work
m.unlock();

If an exception occurs,
unlock() may never execute.

---------------------------------------------------------

8. RAII with File
-----------------

ifstream file("data.txt");

if(file)
{
    // Read file
}

When file goes out of scope,
it is automatically closed.

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Advantages
-------------
• Automatic resource cleanup
• Exception safe
• Prevents memory leaks
• Prevents resource leaks
• Cleaner code
• No manual delete/free/close/unlock
• Easy to maintain

---------------------------------------------------------

10. Disadvantages
-----------------
• Slight overhead for wrapper objects
• Requires proper class design
• Cyclic shared_ptr references can still leak memory

---------------------------------------------------------

11. RAII in STL
---------------
Examples:
• std::unique_ptr
• std::shared_ptr
• std::lock_guard
• std::unique_lock
• std::fstream / ifstream / ofstream
• std::jthread (C++20)

---------------------------------------------------------

12. RAII vs Manual Resource Management
--------------------------------------

Without RAII

int* p = new int(10);

// work

delete p;

Problem:
If an exception occurs before delete,
memory leaks.

--------------------------------------

With RAII

{
    unique_ptr<int> p = make_unique<int>(10);

} // Automatically deleted here.

---------------------------------------------------------

13. Common Interview Questions
------------------------------

Q1. What is RAII?

Resource Acquisition Is Initialization.
Acquire resources in constructor,
release them in destructor.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Why is RAII important?

It prevents resource leaks and provides
automatic cleanup, especially when exceptions occur.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Give examples of RAII.

• unique_ptr
• shared_ptr
• lock_guard
• unique_lock
• fstream
• ifstream
• ofstream
• jthread (C++20)

---------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Which resources can RAII manage?

• Memory
• Files
• Mutexes
• Sockets
• Database connections
• Threads
• Any resource requiring acquire/release

---------------------------------------------------------

Q5. How does RAII provide exception safety?****************************

When stack unwinding occurs due to an exception,
destructors of local objects are automatically called,
releasing all owned resources.

---------------------------------------------------------

14. One-Line Interview Answer
-----------------------------

"RAII is a C++ idiom that ties the lifetime of a resource to the lifetime
of an object. Resources are acquired in the constructor and released in
the destructor, ensuring automatic cleanup, exception safety, and preventing
resource leaks."

